# Google hardware key attestation root certificate
#
# Trusting this certificate is what makes an Android key attestation mean anything: a chain that
# does not lead to one of these roots carries an extension written by whoever produced the chain.
# Adding or replacing a file in this directory widens what the server accepts as hardware backing,
# so review it as a trust decision rather than as a data update.
#
# Subject      : CN=Key Attestation CA1, OU=Android, O=Google LLC, C=US
# Issuer       : CN=Key Attestation CA1, OU=Android, O=Google LLC, C=US  (self-signed)
# Serial       : 84A9D0297B0EB58AE7FF0E80DE760605
# Valid        : Jul 17 22:32:18 2025 GMT .. Jul 15 22:32:18 2035 GMT
# SHA-256 (DER), hex       : 6d9db4ce6c5c0b293166d08986e05774a8776ceb525d9e4329520de12ba4bcc0
# SHA-256 (DER), base64url : bZ20zmxcCykxZtCJhuBXdKh3bOtSXZ5DKVIN4SukvMA
#
# Source    : https://android.googleapis.com/attestation/root   (retrieved 2026-08-26)
# Reference : https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/security-key-attestation
#
# Reproduce and verify:
#   curl -s https://android.googleapis.com/attestation/root | jq -r '.[]' > roots.pem
#   openssl x509 -in this-file.pem -outform DER | openssl dgst -sha256
#   # expected: 6d9db4ce6c5c0b293166d08986e05774a8776ceb525d9e4329520de12ba4bcc0
#
# Rotation: Google publishes new roots without notice (the Key Attestation CA1 root appeared in
# 2025). When a chain is rejected with "does not lead to a trusted root" and the device is genuine,
# re-fetch the source above and add the missing root here. A deployment that cannot wait for a
# release can set client_instance_platform_config.android_key_attestation.trusted_root_certificates
# on the client instead.
#
